# CS509 - Assignment 3 (Individual) - Report

**Task:** Minimum Spanning Tree — Kruskal's algorithm and Prim's algorithm
**Work mode:** Individual
**Input format:** Weighted, undirected adjacency list (Section 5.1), converted to CSR before timing.

## Test Graphs

All graphs were generated with `tools/generate_mst_graph.cpp`: connectivity is guaranteed by a
Hamiltonian path, with extra random edges added to reach an average degree of ~4 (sparse, in the
E ≈ 2V–4V range required by Section 4.2). Positive integer weights, seed = 65.

| File | V | E | Graph type |
|---|---|---|---|
| mst_10.txt | 10 | 29 | Connected, weighted, undirected, sparse |
| mst_100.txt | 100 | 299 | Connected, weighted, undirected, sparse |
| mst_10000.txt | 10,000 | 29,999 | Connected, weighted, undirected, sparse |
| mst_50000.txt | 50,000 | 149,999 | Connected, weighted, undirected, sparse |
| mst_100000.txt | 100,000 | 299,999 | Connected, weighted, undirected, sparse |

## 9.1 MST Results Table

| File | V | E | Exp. Wt. | Kruskal Wt. | Prim Wt. | Kruskal Time | Prim Time | Equal? | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| mst_10.txt | 10 | 29 | n/a | 152 | 152 | 0.002257 ms | 0.002395 ms | Yes | Pass |
| mst_100.txt | 100 | 299 | n/a | 1,619 | 1,619 | 0.021481 ms | 0.023558 ms | Yes | Pass |
| mst_10000.txt | 10,000 | 29,999 | n/a | 193,390 | 193,390 | 1.99762 ms | 3.70410 ms | Yes | Pass |
| mst_50000.txt | 50,000 | 149,999 | n/a | 984,203 | 984,203 | 11.4573 ms | 22.8724 ms | Yes | Pass |
| mst_100000.txt | 100,000 | 299,999 | n/a | 1,962,880 | 1,962,880 | 20.1855 ms | 49.8538 ms | Yes | Pass |

"Exp. Wt." is n/a because these are randomly generated graphs with no independently known
minimum weight; correctness is instead verified by both algorithms agreeing on the same total
weight for every input (see "Equal?" column), which is the check required by Section 2.1.

## Notes

- **Timing:** Only the call to `kruskal_mst()` / `prim_mst()` is timed. Adjacency-list parsing and
  `build_csr()` run before the timer starts, per Section 8. Kruskal's edge extraction and sort run
  inside `kruskal_mst()`, so they are included in its timed section as required.
- **Correctness:** Both algorithms report identical total MST weight on every test file.
- **Scaling:** Kruskal is consistently faster than Prim on these sparse graphs (edge-sort dominates
  Kruskal's cost, while Prim's binary-heap decrease-key-by-reinsertion pattern does more work per
  edge relaxation here). Both run comfortably within a fraction of a second up to V = 100,000.
